{
 "id": "2026-11-17-c",
 "title": "Your body makes 2 million blood cells a SECOND 🩸",
 "description": "In the last second your bone marrow built over two million new red blood cells. Each one is packed with about 270 million hemoglobin molecules, then throws out its own nucleus (no DNA, more room for oxygen), races around your body for about 120 days and is recycled by your spleen and liver, which keep the iron. You carry about 25 trillion of them, so 25 trillion / 120 days = about 2.4 million new ones every second.\n\nSources: Stanford Blood Center; Sender, Fuchs & Milo 2016 (via Science News); Massachusetts General Hospital (2016).",
